    id: "talakadu",
    name: "Talakadu",
    state: "Karnataka",
    hidden: true,
    tagline: "A Thousand-Year-Old Capital, Slowly Swallowed by River Sand",
    description: "Talakadu, on the banks of the Kaveri roughly 45 km east of Mysuru, was once the capital of the Western Ganga dynasty, which ruled this part of Karnataka around a thousand years ago, and grew into a flourishing town with more than 30 temples. Over roughly two centuries, the town was genuinely abandoned and its temples buried under drifting river sand — researchers point to a real, natural combination of the Kaveri shifting course, erosion, and wind-driven sand accumulation as the likely cause, as residents gradually tired of the sand and simply moved away. Local tradition offers a different explanation: legend holds that Alamelamma, wife of a Vijayanagara viceroy, drowned herself in the Kaveri after Raja Wodeyar of Mysore tried to seize her wealth, cursing as she did so that Talakadu would become sand, the nearby Malangi would become a whirlpool, and the Mysore royal line would fail to produce heirs — a story many locals still connect to the real, later difficulties the Wodeyar dynasty had producing direct male heirs. The Archaeological Survey of India began excavating the site only in 1991, uncovering temples including the Pataleshwara, Vaidyeshwara, Maruleshwara, Arkeshwara, and Mallikarjuna shrines, several of which are re-buried in sand between excavations to protect them, and only briefly exposed during the Panchalinga Darshana festival held roughly once every twelve years.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/A_temple_buried_in_the_sand%2C_Talakadu.jpg/1280px-A_temple_buried_in_the_sand%2C_Talakadu.jpg",
