    description: "Linganamakki Dam, built by the Karnataka state government in 1964 across the Sharavathi River in Sagara taluk, Shivamogga district, stretches 2.74 km and holds an effective storage capacity of about 4.29 cubic km — genuinely the primary reservoir for the Sharavathi Hydroelectric Project, one of India's oldest and largest hydroelectric schemes, sitting just 9 km upstream of Jog Falls on the same river. Its construction had a real human cost of displacement: the earlier Madenur (Hirebhaskar) Dam, built 1939-48 on the same river, was submerged when Linganamakki became operational in 1964, and its resident community was relocated to nearby Talaguppa, Kargal, Thumri, and Jog. The dam's most direct practical effect for visitors is genuinely counterintuitive — when its sluice gates are closed for hydroelectric generation, Jog Falls itself runs to little more than a trickle, meaning the two sites are best understood, and often visited, together rather than as separate attractions.",
