    description: "Padmanabhapuram Palace, genuinely the oldest, largest, and best-preserved traditional wooden palace complex in India — and widely regarded as the largest wooden palace in all of Asia — traces its core to 1601 CE, first raised as \"Kalkulathu Koikkal\" under Iravi Varma Kulasekhara Perumal, ruler of Venad from 1592 to 1609. Around 1750, Anizham Tirunal Marthanda Varma rebuilt the palace and its fort, then formally dedicated it to Sree Padmanabha Swamy, the Travancore royal family's own tutelary deity — after which the old Kalkulam palace took the name Padmanabhapuram. It served as the genuine seat of the Travancore kingdom until 1795, when the capital moved to Thiruvananthapuram, and the complex — spread across 6.5 acres and built up in stages from the 1590s through the early 1800s — reopened as a public museum in 1935. Though the palace sits geographically within present-day Tamil Nadu (Kanyakumari district), it remains maintained by Kerala's own Department of Archaeology, a genuine historical enclave reflecting its deep roots in Travancore's — and by extension Kerala's — royal history.",
