    id: "chembra-peak",
    name: "Chembra Peak",
    state: "Kerala",
    hidden: true,
    tagline: "Wayanad's Highest Point, With a Heart-Shaped Lake That Never Runs Dry",
    description: "Chembra Peak, at roughly 2,100 metres, is genuinely the highest point in Wayanad district, part of the Western Ghats and offering real, sweeping panoramic views across the surrounding hills. Its best-known feature, the heart-shaped Hridaya Saras (\"heart lake\"), sits about 1.5 km below the summit and is reached via a roughly 7 km round-trip trek through tea plantations, forest, and open meadows, taking about 2.5 hours each way; the lake carries genuine local mythological significance and, notably, is said to never dry up even in Wayanad's driest summers. The peak's real history extends beyond scenery: its dense jungle and summit vantage point served as guerrilla-war terrain for Pazhassi Raja, the celebrated late-18th-century freedom fighter, in his real armed resistance against the British East India Company — a documented chapter of Wayanad's broader colonial-era history alongside its more recent life as an ecotourism destination.",
