    id: "rakhigarhi",
    name: "Rakhigarhi",
    state: "Haryana",
    tagline: "One of the Five Largest Cities of the Indus Valley Civilization",
    description: "Rakhigarhi is the site of one of the five largest known townships of the Harappan civilization on the Indian subcontinent — the others being Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, and Ganweriwala in Pakistan, and Dholavira in Gujarat. Five interconnected mounds spread across a huge area form the site's unique layout, two of which were thickly populated. Excavated by Shri Amarendra Nath of the Archaeological Survey of India, the site revealed a mature Harappan-phase planned township with mud-brick and burnt-brick houses fitted with a proper drainage system. Its ceramic industry — dish-on-stand pottery, vases, jars, bowls, beakers, and more — sat alongside an animal-sacrifice pit lined with mud brick and triangular and circular fire altars, plus terracotta and shell bangles, semiprecious-stone beads, animal figurines, toy cart frames, bone points, and inscribed steatite seals — genuinely significant material for understanding Harappan life at scale.",
