    description: "Dal Lake, an 18 sq km freshwater lake in Srinagar referenced in ancient Sanskrit texts as \"Mahasarit\" (great lake), took its enduring character under Mughal rule, when emperors laid out the gardens and pavilions that still ring its shores today. Its houseboats — elegant 24–38 metre \"floating palaces\" graded like hotels, a British-era innovation from a time when foreigners couldn't buy Kashmiri land — remain its defining image, each paired with its own shikara, a slender 15-foot paddle boat that doubles as water taxi and floating shop. The lake's real, still-working floating vegetable gardens (locally \"Raad\") bloom with lotus in July–August and grow tomatoes and melons on matted vegetation islands, while the lake itself has genuinely shrunk from roughly 22 to 18 sq km under pollution and eutrophication pressure — a real, ongoing restoration effort backed by government funding runs alongside the tourism it supports.",
