    tagline: "150 Years of Dogra Rule, in a Palace That Has Survived 36 Fires",
    description: "Mubarak Mandi Palace, in Jammu's old walled city overlooking the Tawi River, was begun in 1710 under Raja Dhruv Dev and grew over roughly 150 years into the principal seat of the Dogra dynasty, home to every maharaja from Gulab Singh through to Hari Singh, who relocated to the newer Hari Niwas Palace in 1925. The 12-acre, 25-building complex genuinely blends Rajasthani, Mughal, and European Baroque styles across structures including the Darbar Hall, the round Gol Ghar, the Pink Palace, and the mirror-worked Sheesh Mahal — though its history has been a real, hard one, surviving more than 36 fires including a devastating 1898 blaze that destroyed historical records. Since independence the complex has passed to government control, with part of it now housing the Dogra Art Museum's roughly 800 Kangra, Basholi, and Jammu-school paintings, while restoration work — including the 2025 reopening of the Toshkhana artifact repository — continues on a site still visibly, honestly showing its age.",
