    description: "Hari Niwas Palace, overlooking the Tawi River and the Trikuta hills, was commissioned by Sir Hari Singh, the last reigning Maharaja of Jammu and Kashmir, and completed shortly after 1925 as an extension to the older Amar Mahal Palace next door. A genuine Art Deco structure — smooth stucco facades, geometric relief panels, and ribbon windows adapted to Jammu's subtropical climate — it served as the Maharaja's leisure residence until 1947, when he departed for Mumbai following independence. Converted into a 40-room heritage hotel in 1990 by the royal family's descendants, its Victorian-style interiors, hardwood floors, and a 300-seat Durbar Hall are still genuinely in use today, set amid the same mango groves and tree-lined driveway that have marked the approach since the 1920s.",
