    tagline: "A Mountain-Ringed Lake Where Legend, Faith, and Boating Meet",
    description: "Mansar Lake, in Samba district about 62 km from Jammu, is a roughly 1.6 km by 0.8 km natural lake set in a bowl of low, wooded hills whose reflection settles across the still water at dawn and dusk. The lake carries a real place in local legend, associated in the Mahabharata with Babruvahana, and is home to a small shrine to Sheshnaag, marked by iron chains where pilgrims traditionally toss offerings; several Shiva, Narasimha, and Durga temples ring the shore. A well-known and genuinely charming local tradition has pilgrims feeding the lake's resident fish — believed to be a form of the serpent deity's blessing — and newlywed couples and families performing a customary first circuit of the lake, alongside a first-haircut (mundan) ceremony for infants. Paddle boats are available on the water, and the surrounding hills make for an easy, unhurried half-day outing from Jammu.",
