    name: "Krimchi Temples",
    state: "Jammu and Kashmir (UT)",
    tagline: "Seven Stone Shikharas on a Stream Where the Pandavas Are Said to Have Sheltered",
    description: "The Krimchi Temples stand on the banks of the Birunala stream in Krimchi village, about 12 km from Udhampur, a compact group of seven Nagara-style stone shrines — four larger temples and three smaller ones — that the Archaeological Survey of India dates to the 8th–9th century AD. Their rekha-style shikhara towers, panchratha ground plans, and chaitya-window ornamentation reflect a Gurjara-Pratihara architectural tradition adapted to heavy Himalayan sandstone, with restrained carving and simplified mouldings suited to the local stone. The principal shrine, roughly 50 feet tall, is dedicated to Shiva, with subsidiary shrines to Ganesha, Vishnu, and Parvati; two of the seven structures were damaged centuries ago and stand as ruins alongside the intact ones. Locally the complex is called \"Pandav Mandir,\" tied to a legend that King Kichak founded the town and that the Pandavas sheltered here during their exile — a genuinely evocative, still lightly-visited site now under ASI protection.",
