    description: "Vishansar Lake sits at roughly 3,710 metres (about 12,170 feet) near Ganderbal town and Sonamarg, its name meaning \"lake of Vishnu\" in Kashmiri and carrying real religious significance for Kashmiri Pandits. An oligotrophic alpine lake about a kilometre long, it lies frozen from December to April and, in summer, opens into a strikingly clear, mirror-still stretch of water surrounded by lush meadows where local shepherds graze sheep and goats — the source of the brown trout that live in its waters. Vishansar feeds into the neighbouring Krishansar Lake and connects onward toward Gadsar Lake across the Gadsar Pass, making it one of the central waypoints on the celebrated Kashmir Great Lakes Trek, and its waters are the ultimate source of the Kishanganga River. It is reached only on foot, as part of a multi-day high-altitude trek rather than a drive-up destination — a genuinely remote, high-Himalayan sight rather than an easy day trip.",
