    tagline: "A Hilltop Rajput-Style Fort Rebuilt Across 1,300 Years, Near the Vaishno Devi Route",
    description: "Bhimgarh Fort sits on a roughly 150-metre hillock near Reasi, about 64 km northwest of Jammu, its long, bastioned stone walls tracing a construction history that runs from the 8th century, when Raja Bhim Dev first built it in mud and clay, through a stone reconstruction under Raja Rishipal Rana, reinforcement by Maan Singh and the warrior Bhadur Singh in the early 18th century, and a comprehensive rebuilding under Maharaja Gulab Singh between 1817 and 1841 after he took control from the Sikh Empire, overseen by his Diwan, Amir Chand. Modelled loosely on the twenty-four Rajput forts of Rajasthan, the fort's stone masonry incorporates arrow-slits and rounded bastions, three sequential gates, internal chambers, a temple to Bhim Devta, a water tank, and a twelve-doorway pavilion with panoramic views over the Reasi valley. Handed to the State Archaeology Department in 1989 and renovated with added gardens and pathways by the Vaishno Devi Shrine Board in 1990, the fort makes a genuinely worthwhile detour for travellers already passing through Reasi on the way to the Vaishno Devi pilgrimage.",
