    tagline: "Twelve Terraces of Water and Chinar Trees, Built by an Emperor's Own Father-in-Law",
    description: "Nishat Bagh, on the eastern shore of Dal Lake near Srinagar, was built in 1633 by Asif Khan, brother of Empress Nur Jahan and father-in-law to Shah Jahan — a courtier's garden rather than an imperial one, which is genuinely why it has only two sections (public and zenana) instead of the four of its more famous neighbour, Shalimar Bagh. At 19 hectares, it is Kashmir's second-largest Mughal garden, its name meaning \"Garden of Joy,\" and it rises from the lakeshore in twelve descending terraces said to represent the twelve zodiacal signs, each linked by a 4-metre-wide stone water channel with fountains and pools, lined the whole way up with avenues of chinar and cypress. A well-told local story has Shah Jahan cutting off the garden's water supply after Asif Khan declined to gift it to him outright — reversed only after the emperor learned a loyal servant had secretly tried to restore the flow. Today it's run by the J&K Tourism Department and remains one of the most visited of Srinagar's Mughal gardens, its Zabarwan Range backdrop turning gold and red with the chinars each autumn.",
