    tagline: "A Mughal Emperor's Own Pleasure Garden, Rebuilt by Three Rulers Across Three Centuries",
    description: "Shalimar Bagh sits at the northeast edge of Dal Lake, its history genuinely layered across rulers: Emperor Jahangir enlarged an older garden here in 1619 and named it \"Farah Baksh\" (the delightful); Shah Jahan's governor Zafar Khan extended it further in 1630 as \"Faiz Baksh\" (the bountiful); and later Sikh governors and Dogra maharajas kept it as a royal pleasure ground under the name that stuck, Shalimar. At 12.4 hectares, its three terraces are linked by the 587-metre Shah Nahar water channel and include a Diwan-e-Aam (public hall), Diwan-e-Khas (private audience chamber), a zenana garden, and — its most celebrated feature — a black-marble baradari pavilion built for Shah Jahan, its arched niches once lit by oil lamps behind cascading water. With some 410 fountains across the grounds and avenues of chinar trees that turn genuinely spectacular in spring and autumn, it remains, alongside Nishat Bagh, one of the two great Mughal gardens on Dal Lake's shore.",
