    state: "Jammu and Kashmir (UT)",
    tagline: "The Smallest, Most Charming Mughal Garden, Built Around a Spring With a Royal Reputation",
    description: "Chashme Shahi, set in the Zabarwan Range near Raj Bhawan overlooking Dal Lake, is the smallest but by many accounts the most architecturally charming of Srinagar's Mughal gardens — one English traveller called it exactly that. Built in 1632 by Ali Mardan Khan, a governor under Shah Jahan, on the emperor's orders as a gift for his eldest son Prince Dara Shikoh, the garden centres on a natural spring whose water was known long before the Mughals arrived — discovered, tradition holds, by the revered Kashmiri Pandit saint Rupa Bhawani, after whom it was first called \"Chashme Sahibi\" before the name shifted to \"Chashme Shahi,\" the royal spring. At just under an acre, the garden's three small terraces carry the spring water through an aqueduct, a waterfall, and a fountain, marked at its source by a two-storey Kashmiri hut. The spring's water is genuinely reputed for its taste and mineral qualities — famously, Jawaharlal Nehru had it shipped to Delhi — and the garden is open March through November, with the best blooms in May and June.",
