    tagline: "A Hilltop Shiva Shrine Where Adi Shankara Is Said to Have Composed His Hymns",
    description: "Shankaracharya Temple sits atop a hill in the Zabarwan Range, roughly 1,000 feet above the Srinagar valley floor, its origins genuinely disputed — some trace it to King Gopaditya around 371 BCE, others to the 6th-century Hun ruler Mihirakula — but its enduring name comes from a strong Kashmiri Hindu tradition that the philosopher Adi Shankara visited and composed his Saundarya Lahari here. An ASI-protected monument, the temple's octagonal stone base rises about 20 feet before opening into a square chamber held up by four columns, reached by roughly 240 steps up a walled staircase; inside, in a small dark circular chamber, sits a Shiva lingam encircled by a carved serpent. The temple genuinely commands one of the best panoramic views over Srinagar and Dal Lake, and draws its biggest crowds during Maha Shivaratri, when it's lit up for the festival.",
