    description: "Hazratbal Shrine, on Dal Lake's northern shore, is widely regarded as the holiest Islamic site in Kashmir, its white marble dome and slender minaret genuinely among Srinagar's most recognizable landmarks. It houses the Moi-e-Muqqadas, a relic believed to be a strand of hair of the Prophet Muhammad, brought from Medina to Bijapur in 1635 by Syed Abdullah Madani and later carried to Kashmir around 1700, where Inayat Begum established the shrine and began a custodian lineage — the Banday family — that continues to this day; the relic itself is displayed to the public only on specific religious occasions such as Mawlid. The original structure dates to the 1630s under Shah Jahan, but the shrine seen today largely reflects a 1970s reconstruction led by Sheikh Abdullah that added its marble dome and minaret, followed by a more recent beautification project between 2017 and 2024 incorporating traditional Kashmiri woodwork. It functions as both an active mosque and a major pilgrimage site, its lawns overlooking Dal Lake genuinely one of Srinagar's most peaceful public spaces.",
