    id: "wular-lake",
    name: "Wular Lake",
    state: "Jammu and Kashmir (UT)",
    tagline: "One of Asia's Largest Freshwater Lakes, Fed by the Jhelum and a 15th-Century Island",
    description: "Wular Lake, near Bandipora town, is one of the largest freshwater lakes in the Indian subcontinent, its surface area swelling seasonally anywhere from 30 to as much as 189 square kilometres, fed primarily by the Jhelum River along with the Madhumati and Arin streams. Formed by tectonic activity, the lake holds a single island, Zainul Lank, built in 1444 by the Kashmiri sultan Zain-ul-Abidin, and has been recognized as a Ramsar Wetland since 1990 and a Wetland of National Importance since 1986. It genuinely supports a working local economy — fishing for common carp, rosy barb, and snowtrout, along with harvesting of aquatic plants for food and fodder — and draws a wide range of birdlife including black-eared kites, Eurasian sparrowhawks, and short-toed eagles. The lake faces real, acknowledged pressures — pollution, agricultural runoff, and weed infestation among them — and is the subject of an ongoing, funded restoration effort under the National Lake Conservation Programme, alongside the long-contested proposed Tulbul Lock Project tied to the India-Pakistan Indus Waters Treaty.",
