    description: "Bahu Fort stands on a rocky plateau overlooking the Tawi River, about 5 km from Jammu's city centre, its origins tied by tradition to Bahu, eldest of Agnigarbha's eighteen sons and the legendary founder of Jammu city itself — making it, by local reckoning, the oldest fort in the city. The structure seen today owes most of its form to a 1585 reconstruction under Autar Dev and later work by Maharaja Gulab Singh and Maharaja Ranbir Singh, its thick sandstone walls in lime-and-brick mortar rising into eight octagonal towers, with a main gate wide enough for elephant passage and a pyramidal ammunition store hiding an underground prison cell with a secret escape route. Within the fort walls sits the genuinely popular Mahakali (Bawe Wali Mata) temple, its white marble mandapa housing an image of the goddess said to have been brought from Ayodhya, drawing steady crowds especially on Tuesdays and Sundays and, notably, a resident population of rhesus monkeys around the grounds. The surrounding forest has been developed into Bagh-e-Bahu, a landscaped park with sweeping views over Jammu city — a genuinely pleasant place to end a fort visit.",
