    tagline: "A 134km Himalayan Lake That Turns Every Shade of Blue, Two-Thirds in Tibet",
    description: "Pangong Tso stretches 134 km across the Changthang plateau, from Leh district in India into Tibet's Rutog County — roughly two-thirds of it lies in Chinese-administered territory, with the Line of Actual Control running straight through the water, a real and still-active geopolitical fact that shaped the 1962 war and the 2020 border standoff. Sitting at about 4,300 metres, it's among the highest brackish-water lakes on earth: the eastern basin is only mildly saline, the western stretch far saltier, yet the whole lake still freezes solid enough each winter for an annual frozen-lake marathon. What draws most visitors, though, is the water itself — an ever-shifting run of blues and turquoise that changes through the day with the light, framed by bare, folded mountains with almost no vegetation. The lake's profile rose sharply after it featured in the Bollywood film 3 Idiots, and the resulting tourist boom has brought real environmental strain to its shoreline — a trade-off worth being mindful of on a visit.",
