    description: "Tso Kar sits at 4,530 metres on the Rupshu Plateau in Ladakh's remote Changthang region, its name — \"white lake\" — coming honestly from the pale salt efflorescence crusting its margins as water evaporates. Changpa nomads once harvested this salt for trade with Tibet, a livelihood only recently fallen away, and the lake and its surrounding wetland complex were recognized as India's 42nd Ramsar site in December 2020. Tso Kar is genuinely one of the country's most important breeding grounds for the black-necked crane, and the wider basin regularly turns up kiang (Tibetan wild asses), Tibetan gazelles, snow leopards, and saker falcons roaming open grassland against a backdrop of snow-streaked peaks. Reached via a roughly 250 km road from Leh, it sees far fewer visitors than Pangong Tso, making it a genuinely quieter, wildlife-focused alternative for travellers willing to go a little further.",
