    description: "Tso Moriri sits at 4,522 metres in the Changthang Plateau, a 26 km-long lake covering some 13,500 hectares and reaching depths of 105 metres — genuinely one of the highest lakes of its size anywhere in the world, and among the least disturbed. Designated a Ramsar Wetland in 2002, it supports real ecological weight: 34 recorded bird species including endangered black-necked cranes and bar-headed geese, alongside snow leopards, Tibetan wolves, Himalayan blue sheep, marmots, and kiang across the surrounding grassland. On its northwest shore sits Korzok, a year-round settlement and the highest permanently inhabited village in the region, home to the roughly 400-year-old Korzok Monastery — a real pilgrimage site as much as a scenic one, drawing both Buddhist devotees and travellers. Reached by a genuinely long drive from Leh (219–240 km), Tso Moriri rewards the extra distance with a stillness and ecological intactness that busier lakes on the tourist circuit have largely lost.",
