    description: "Alchi Monastery sits on the south bank of the Indus at 3,100 metres, about 65 km west of Leh, and is genuinely unlike Ladakh's other great monasteries in one important way: it isn't built on a dramatic clifftop, but on flat ground within a quiet village, its low profile hiding some of the oldest surviving Buddhist paintings in the region. Tradition credits the 11th-century scholar-translator Rinchen Zangpo as founder, though modern inscription analysis points to an unnamed uncle of the patron Tsültrim Ö building the complex's Dukhang (Assembly Hall) no earlier than 1140 CE. The three main shrines — Dukhang, Sumtseg, and the Manjushri Temple — combine loam-and-stone Tibetan construction with genuinely luxuriant woodwork carved by Kashmiri craftsmen, and the walls carry murals reflecting both Buddhist and Hindu court life of the period, including a royal drinking scene dated to around 1200 CE. Administered today by nearby Likir Monastery as part of the wider Alchi group of monuments, it stands as one of the most complete surviving windows into trans-Himalayan Buddhist art anywhere.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Alchi_Monastery%2C_Leh%2C_Ladakh%2C_India_01.jpg/1280px-Alchi_Monastery%2C_Leh%2C_Ladakh%2C_India_01.jpg",
