    description: "Hemis Monastery, on the Indus riverbank about 45 km from Leh along the Leh–Manali Highway, is the principal seat of the Drukpa Kagyu lineage of Tibetan Buddhism and, by most accounts, the wealthiest and best-endowed monastery in Ladakh. Though a site here predates the 11th century, the monastery as it stands was re-established in 1672 by King Sengge Namgyal, and its 17th-century courtyard gallery holds a genuinely striking set of paintings depicting the Eighty-Four Mahasiddhas, rendered in mineral pigments that have kept their colour for centuries. Its best-known event, the annual Hemis Festival each June, honours Padmasambhava (Guru Rinpoche) with tantric cham mask dances performed to cymbals, large drums, and long trumpets — one of the most elaborate monastic festivals in the region. The monastery also carries an odd footnote in Western pop history: an 1894 claim by Russian journalist Nicolas Notovitch that he'd found a manuscript here describing Jesus's travels to India has been thoroughly debunked by scholars, including biblical historian Bart Ehrman, but the story still circulates.",
