    tagline: "A 19,024-Foot Road Built by the Army in 2017, Once the World's Highest",
    description: "Umling La, on a ridgeline dividing the Koyul Lungpa and Indus River in Ladakh's remote Changthang region near Demchok, sits at 19,024 feet (5,799 metres) — genuinely higher than Everest's South Base Camp, in conditions that hit -40°C and roughly half the sea-level oxygen. The Chisumle–Umling La–Demchok road was built in 2017 by the Border Roads Organisation, a serious engineering achievement in extreme high-altitude terrain, and from 2017 until 2025 it held the title of the world's highest motorable road — a title it genuinely lost in 2025 when India completed an even higher route over Mig La at 19,400 feet, worth knowing so you don't repeat an outdated claim. It remains, regardless, a real bucket-list destination for high-altitude riders and drivers, a roughly 230–250 km, 8–10 hour journey from Leh through some of the most extreme terrain any public road in the world reaches.",
