    description: "Nubra Valley sits at over 3,000 metres north of Leh, its headquarters town Diskit reached by a 120 km drive that crosses Khardung La — historically part of the Silk Road trade route into western China's Xinjiang and Central Asia, with caravans of wool, silk, spices, cotton, and indigo once passing through regularly. Its most surreal feature sits between the villages of Hunder and Diskit: roughly 7 km of genuine sand dunes, a real cold-desert landscape at high altitude, where double-humped Bactrian camels — descendants of Silk Road pack animals — graze in surrounding seabuckthorn forest and are available for tourist rides. Diskit itself is home to the dramatically hillside-set Diskit Monastery, founded in the 14th century, its defining landmark a 32-metre Maitreya Buddha statue overlooking the valley below — genuinely one of the most striking single images in all of Ladakh.",
