    id: "suru-valley",
    name: "Suru Valley",
    state: "Ladakh (UT)",
    tagline: "A Shia Muslim Valley in the Shadow of the 7,000m Nun-Kun Massif",
    description: "Suru Valley, drained by the Suru River in Kargil district, is genuinely one of Ladakh's most distinct regions — home to roughly 25,000 Purgi people of Tibetan descent, the great majority of them followers of Shia Islam, with the Purgi language spoken by about 95% of the population around the valley's main town, Sankoo. It sits in real visual dialogue with the twin, snow-capped peaks of the Nun-Kun massif, which top 7,000 metres and are visible from multiple points along the valley — the village of Tangole serves as a starting point for mountaineering expeditions up the range. Further up the valley near Rangdum, the culture shifts again to a small Buddhist community supporting the 18th-century Rangdum Monastery, making Suru a genuinely layered valley religiously and culturally in a way few single Ladakh destinations are. The NH301 Kargil–Padum highway runs its length, connecting Suru to both Kargil and, ultimately, Zanskar beyond.",
