    id: "zanskar-valley",
    name: "Zanskar Valley",
    state: "Ladakh (UT)",
    tagline: "A Region Closed to Foreigners Until 1974, Home to the Frozen-River Chadar Trek",
    description: "Zanskar sits some 250 km south of Kargil, its remote valleys generally between 3,600 and 4,000 metres — among the coldest continuously inhabited places on earth — and its isolation was real and recent: the region stayed closed to foreign visitors until 1974 owing to border sensitivities. Padum, the district headquarters (since April 2026) and largest settlement, holds under 700 residents but now has hotels, homestays, and restaurants for the tourists who do make the journey. Zanskar's best-known claim to fame is the Chadar trek, which follows the Zanskar River's frozen winter gorge past Padum — a genuinely serious, cold-weather trekking route that for generations was locals' only winter link to the outside world. The valley's Buddhist heritage runs deep, with 93.91% of the population Buddhist and a monastery in nearly every village, including the notable Phugtal, Karsha, Bardan, and Rangdum gompas; roads to Zanskar typically run only June through September before winter isolation sets back in.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Padum_Zanskar_View_From_Karsha_Oct22_A7C_03984.jpg/1280px-Padum_Zanskar_View_From_Karsha_Oct22_A7C_03984.jpg",
    heroImage: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Padum_Zanskar_View_From_Karsha_Oct22_A7C_03984.jpg/1280px-Padum_Zanskar_View_From_Karsha_Oct22_A7C_03984.jpg",
