    description: "Sham Valley, the lower stretch of western Ladakh starting around Likir village roughly 58 km from Leh, is genuinely known as the region's \"baby trek\" — an easy, roughly 32 km, three-day route with a maximum elevation of about 3,874 metres, gentle enough for first-time high-altitude trekkers while still passing through real, characterful Ladakhi countryside. The route typically begins at Likir Monastery, a striking hilltop gompa, and threads through Yangthang village amid barley fields and willow groves, then Hemis Shukpachan, celebrated for its juniper groves and often named among Ladakh's prettiest villages, before reaching Tingmosgang with its own old monastery. Many itineraries loop back via Alchi, allowing a stop at its famous 11th-century Kashmiri-style murals on the return to Leh. Sham Valley is as much a window into everyday rural Ladakhi life — apricot orchards, working farms, village monasteries — as it is a trekking destination, making it a genuinely good introduction to the region beyond its headline lakes and passes.",
