    state: "Ladakh (UT)",
    tagline: "A Balti Village on the Shyok River, Under Pakistani Rule Until 1971",
    description: "Turtuk sits on the banks of the Shyok River, sandwiched between the Karakoram Range and the Himalayas, in the Nubra district near the Line of Control. Its history is genuinely unusual for an Indian village: it remained under Pakistani administration after the 1947–48 war until India's Ladakh Scouts captured it in the 1971 Battle of Turtuk, ending roughly two decades of Pakistani control. That history left Turtuk as one of only five Balti-populated villages under Indian administration, its residents speaking Balti alongside Ladakhi and Urdu and preserving customs distinct from the rest of Ladakh — a genuinely different cultural texture from the Buddhist-majority region around it. The village is also known, with real justification, for its apricots, grown across terraced orchards that form a real part of the local economy and identity, and its proximity to the Siachen Glacier gateway gives it a quiet, still-recent frontier feel.",
