    tagline: "Home to India's First Dark Sky Reserve and the World's Highest Major Observatory",
    description: "Hanle sits at roughly 4,500 metres in eastern Ladakh's Changthang region, near the Chinese border and about 254 km — a genuine 9–10 hour drive — from Leh via Upshi, Chumathang, and Nyoma. It's home to the Indian Astronomical Observatory, run by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics and among the highest-altitude major observatories anywhere in the world, its telescope domes standing stark against the Changthang plateau. In September 2022, the surrounding roughly 1,073 square kilometres — falling within the Changthang Wildlife Sanctuary — was designated the Hanle Dark Sky Reserve, India's first, rated \"excellent\" (Bortle Black) for its near-total absence of light pollution. Hanle village itself, with its own old monastery, remains a genuine, lived-in Changthang settlement rather than a purpose-built tourist stop, making an overnight stay here as much about the remote high-altitude landscape and its Changpa nomad culture as the stargazing that draws most visitors.",
