    description: "Hemis National Park, about 50 km south of Leh, is genuinely India's largest national park, spreading across some 4,400 square kilometres between 3,000 and 6,000 metres of elevation. Its real claim to global significance is snow leopards: with an estimated 200 individuals, Hemis holds the highest recorded density of the species in any protected area on earth, making it one of the best places anywhere to attempt a sighting — realistically in late winter, when the cats descend to lower elevations and are easier to track. The park takes its name from Hemis Monastery, which sits on its edge, and its terrain is genuinely dramatic, with colour-banded mountainsides, the Sumdo Nala stream valley, and habitat for bharal (blue sheep) — the snow leopard's primary prey — alongside ibex and other high-altitude wildlife. Trekking through the park runs mid-June to mid-October, overlapping with, but distinct from, the specialist winter snow-leopard-tracking season.",
