    tagline: "A 5,520-Metre Sacred Rock Pinnacle Where Climbing Is Forbidden",
    description: "Gonbo Rangjon rises dramatically from the Lungnak Valley in Zanskar, a standalone granite pinnacle reaching 5,520 metres — towering more than 1,000 metres above its 4,500-metre base — about 16 km south of Kargyak, the first village reached in Zanskar after crossing the Shingo La pass from Himachal Pradesh. Its name translates to \"Gonbo's Throne,\" a reference to Mahakala (Gonbo), the fierce protector deity of Tibetan Buddhism, and local tradition holds the peak to be his literal abode — a sacred site where pilgrims come to seek blessings and meditate rather than to climb. The mountain also carries an association with Guru Padmasambhava, the 8th-century saint credited with bringing Tantric Buddhism to the Himalayas, and out of genuine religious respect, climbing the peak itself is prohibited. It stands today as one of the most visually striking single landmarks on the Zanskar/Padum–Darcha road, a real geological and spiritual anchor for travellers passing through this remote corner of Ladakh.",
