    tagline: "A Memorial at Drass Honouring the 1999 Kargil War's Fallen Soldiers",
    description: "The Kargil War Memorial sits in Drass, about 5 km from Kargil city on the Srinagar–Leh Highway, built to honour the soldiers who fell in the 1999 Kargil War — one of independent India's most significant military engagements. During the winter of 1998–99, Pakistani forces crossed the Line of Control and occupied strategic heights above Drass and Kargil; India's response, Operation Vijay, launched in May 1999, fought fierce, prolonged battles across brutal high-altitude terrain to reclaim them, a campaign India marks each year on 26 July as Kargil Vijay Diwas. The memorial itself centres on a pink sandstone wall bearing a brass plate engraved with the names of the fallen, alongside a gallery honouring Param Vir Chakra recipient Captain Manoj Pandey, artillery pieces on display, and a giant 11.4 by 7.6-metre national flag flown from a 30-metre pole, installed in 2012. From the site, visitors can look up at Tololing Heights, Tiger Hill, and Point 4875 — the actual peaks fought over — a genuinely sobering, real vantage point on the war's terrain; the memorial drew roughly 125,000 visitors in a single recent year.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Kargil_War_Memorial_Drass.jpg/1280px-Kargil_War_Memorial_Drass.jpg",
