    description: "The Markha Valley Trek follows the Markha River — a tributary of the Zanskar formed at the junction of the Langtang Chu and Nimaling Chu — through villages including Skiu, Markha, Umlung, and Hankar, genuinely one of the most popular multi-day trekking routes anywhere in Ladakh. The classic route runs between two passes: Ganda La near Spituk on the western end and Kongmaru La (Gongmaru La) near Hemis on the eastern end, crossing a landscape of nomadic yak-herding pasture and Buddhist heritage, with Techa Monastery the valley's most significant religious site. At its upper reaches the valley opens toward Kang Yatze, a striking 6,400-metre peak that dominates the skyline near Nimaling, and — genuinely notable for conservation — the entire Markha River corridor lies within Hemis National Park, one of the highest-density snow leopard habitats on earth, giving the trek real ecological as well as scenic weight.",
