    description: "Betla National Park sits within the Palamau Tiger Reserve in Latehar and Palamu districts, one of the very first reserves brought under Project Tiger back in 1974, before formal national park status followed in 1986. It covers 226 square kilometres of sal and bamboo forest — with a 53 sq km tourist zone in the buffer area — genuinely rich in wildlife: tigers and leopards still roam the reserve alongside Indian elephants, gaur (wild bison), sambar and axis deer, wild boar, and sloth bears, with primates like langurs and rhesus monkeys common along the trails. Birdlife is a real draw too, from hornbills and peafowl to crested serpent-eagles and the rare forest owlet. About 170 km northwest of Ranchi, Betla remains one of the most accessible places in eastern India to genuinely encounter central Indian forest wildlife, with jeep safaris the standard way to explore its buffer zone.",
