    tagline: "A Real Elephant Migration Corridor Overlooking Jamshedpur",
    description: "Dalma Wildlife Sanctuary spreads across the Dalma Hills over roughly 195 square kilometres straddling East Singhbhum and Saraikela Kharsawan districts, its nearest city Jamshedpur just below. The sanctuary is genuinely built around its elephants — an estimated 85 as of 2024 — which follow a real seasonal migration, moving into West Bengal each August and September before returning around December and January, making winter the more reliable season for sightings. Beyond elephants, Dalma supports sloth bears, Indian giant squirrels, pangolins, several deer species, and rich birdlife including peacocks, hornbills, and kingfishers; a tiger was even documented in the sanctuary as recently as January 2025. A hilltop Shiva temple within the sanctuary adds a pilgrimage dimension to what's otherwise a straightforward, accessible wildlife and forest destination right on the edge of a major industrial city.",
