    name: "Palamau Fort",
    state: "Jharkhand",
    tagline: "Twin Chero-Dynasty Forts on the Auranga River, Taken by the Mughals in 1660",
    description: "Palamau Fort is actually two forts — an old fort down in the plains and a newer one on an adjoining hill — standing on the bank of the Auranga River in Latehar district, about 28 km from Daltonganj and just 3 km from Betla National Park. Built and expanded under Chero dynasty rulers Anant Rai (who died in 1619) and his successor Medini Rai, the fort served as the seat of Chero power for roughly two centuries starting around 1613 CE. Its history has a real, documented endpoint: Mughal forces under Daud Khan invaded on 3 April 1660 and, after a sustained siege, captured the fort on 9 December 1660, ending independent Chero rule in the region. What remains today are genuinely atmospheric ruins — weathered stone gateways and walls reclaimed by forest — making it a natural pairing with a Betla National Park visit given the short distance between them.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Palamu_Fort%2C_Palamu%2C_Jharkhand_01.jpg/1280px-Palamu_Fort%2C_Palamu%2C_Jharkhand_01.jpg",
