    tagline: "Mud-Walled Village Homes Hand-Painted in an Ancient Tribal Mural Tradition",
    description: "Villages around Hazaribagh, most notably Isko, are home to Sohrai and Khovar painting — an ancient wall-mural tradition practised by Munda, Kurmi, Santhal, and other communities of the region, in which village women hand-paint the mud walls of their homes with natural earth pigments in bold, flowing depictions of animals, birds, flowers, and geometric patterns. Traditionally created around harvest festivals and weddings using fingers, twigs, and cloth swabs dipped in kaolin white, ochre, and manganese-black clays, the art has genuine archaeological roots tracing back to the prehistoric rock art of the surrounding Hazaribagh region, and has since earned a Geographical Indication tag as a distinct regional craft. Visiting the painted village homes offers a rare, still-living glimpse of a tribal art form practised directly on the walls people inhabit rather than confined to a gallery or museum.",
