    tagline: "Twin Ruined Mughal-Era Forts Swallowed by the Forests of Betla National Park",
    description: "Palamu Fort actually comprises two adjoining forts — an Old Fort and a New Fort — built by the Chero dynasty rulers in the 16th and 17th centuries near Daltonganj, their weathered stone gateways, arched entrances, and crumbling ramparts now thoroughly reclaimed by the surrounding forest of Betla National Park. The New Fort's grand entrance archway, flanked by twin bastions and richly carved with floral and geometric motifs in the Mughal architectural idiom, remains the site's most striking surviving structure, its ochre stone stained dark with age and half-covered in creeping vegetation. Set deep within tiger-reserve forest and rarely crowded, Palamu Fort offers a genuinely atmospheric combination of Mughal-era history and reclaimed wilderness, its silence and greenery giving the ruins a haunting, timeless quality distinct from more heavily visited forts elsewhere in India.",
