    description: "Chitradurga Fort — affectionately called Kallina Kote, the \"Stone Fort\" — sprawls across a cluster of rocky hills in central Karnataka, one of the largest and most dramatic hill forts in South India. The town's name itself comes from the landscape: chitra (art) and durga (fort), for the enormous granite boulders around it that naturally resemble elephants, crocodiles, and boats. Built and expanded over centuries, most notably under the Nayakas of Chitradurga, the fort climbs across several hills through a maze of concentric stone walls, ancient gateways, watchtowers, temples, and cleverly engineered water reservoirs, with hidden passages that once helped defend the kingdom. It is bound up with the legend of Onake Obavva, the woman who is said to have single-handedly held off enemy soldiers slipping through a narrow rock cleft — the Onake Obavva Kindi — armed only with a wooden pestle. Walking its ramparts among the boulder-strewn slopes, the fort feels at once historic and genuinely adventurous.",
