    description: "Udupi, a coastal town in Dakshina Kannada country carved into its own district in 1997, is one of South India's most important pilgrimage centres and the spiritual seat founded by the 13th-century philosopher-saint Madhvacharya. Its heart is the Sri Krishna Matha, where the deity of Lord Krishna is worshipped and — uniquely — viewed by devotees through a silver-plated, nine-holed window called the Kanakana Kindi, born of the legend of the devotee Kanakadasa. The temple is run through the Paryaya system, in which the headship rotates every two years among the eight matha (Ashta Mathas) in a grand festival, and the surrounding streets hum with temple ritual, math kitchens, and the town's famous vegetarian food — Udupi is widely regarded as the birthplace of the classic masala dosa and the broader \"Udupi cuisine\" now found across the world. Just beyond the temple town lie some of Karnataka's finest coastal draws: Malpe Beach and its harbour, the boat ride to St. Mary's Island with its rare hexagonal basalt columns, and the lighthouse beach at Kaup.",
