    description: "Dharmasthala, on the banks of the Netravati river in the forested Western Ghats of Dakshina Kannada, is among the most visited pilgrimage centres in Karnataka — and one of India's most unusual in its structure. The Manjunatha Temple at its heart is a Shaivite shrine where Lord Manjunatha (Shiva) is worshipped, yet its priests are Madhwa Vaishnavites and the whole institution has been administered for roughly eight centuries by a hereditary Jain family, the Heggades. A Jain Tirthankara and Lord Manjunatha are venerated on the same consecrated ground, and every visitor — regardless of caste or religion — is served a free meal through the tradition of Annadana. On a hill near the temple stands a 39-foot monolithic statue of Lord Bahubali, a symbol of non-violence and sacrifice. Beyond worship, the town holds the Manjusha Museum of palm-leaf manuscripts and antiques, a Car Museum of vintage automobiles, and the white-marble Chandranatha Swamy Basadi, while the annual Laksha Deepotsava lights a hundred thousand lamps each November–December.",
