    description: "Bidar, on the northern edge of the Deccan Plateau, was successively the capital of the Bahmani, Barid Shahi and other dynasties, and its skyline is still shaped by their monuments — a rich mix of Hindu, Turkish, and Persian architectural styles that earns it the nickname \"city of whispering monuments.\" The 15th-century Bidar Fort is among the most formidable in India, ringed by a triple moat cut into laterite rock and entered through a sequence of great gateways, with the jewel-like Rangin Mahal (Palace of Colours) and its mother-of-pearl and tilework inside. Beyond the fort lie the towering Bahmani Tombs at Ashtur, decorated with fine stucco and painted interiors; Nanak Jhira Sahib, a revered Sikh gurudwara built around a spring linked to Guru Nanak's visit; and the Papnash Shiva Temple and Narasimha Jharna cave shrine. Bidar is also the home of Bidriware — the distinctive craft of silver inlay on blackened metal — which visitors can watch being made and buy across the old city.",
