    description: "Melukote sits on a rocky hill in Mandya district, about 50 km from Mysuru — a small pilgrimage town so bound up with Sri Vaishnava tradition that it is sometimes called Thirunarayanapuram. The 12th-century philosopher Sri Ramanujacharya lived and taught here for years, and the town still runs on that inheritance: the Cheluvanarayana Swamy Temple (Thirunarayana) on the lower town and the Yoganarasimha Swamy Temple crowning the hilltop draw steady streams of devotees, while Sanskrit schools, agraharas (rows of traditional Brahmin homes), and Vedic recitation keep the scholarly atmosphere alive. The stepped Kalyani, a large sacred tank ringed by carved stone pillars and pavilions, is Melukote's most photographed spot. The town's grandest moment is the annual Vairamudi festival, when the deity is adorned with a diamond crown said to belong to the temple's ancient treasury. Melukote is also known for its handloom weaving and its distinctive puliyogare (tamarind rice).",
