    id: "agumbe",
    name: "Agumbe",
    state: "Karnataka",
    tagline: "The 'Cherrapunji of the South' — Rainforest, King Cobras, and Sunsets",
    description: "Agumbe is a tiny village on the crest of the Western Ghats in Shivamogga district, wrapped in some of the densest rainforest in Karnataka and drenched by one of the highest rainfalls in the country — hence its nickname, the \"Cherrapunji of the South.\" It is best known among naturalists as prime King Cobra habitat and the site of a long-running rainforest research station, and its forests have featured in international wildlife documentaries. For most visitors the draw is the Sunset Point, where on clear evenings the view runs across ridge after forested ridge toward the distant Arabian Sea. Around the village lie the Someshwara Wildlife Sanctuary, the ancient Jain temple on Kundadri Hill, and monsoon-fed waterfalls such as Onake Abbi and Barkana. Malnad cuisine, misty mornings, and slow rainforest walks with local guides round out the experience.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/View_from_sunset_point%2C_Agumbe_%2816017939927%29.jpg/1280px-View_from_sunset_point%2C_Agumbe_%2816017939927%29.jpg",
