    id: "nanjangud-srikanteshwara-temple",
    name: "Nanjangud (Srikanteshwara Temple)",
    state: "Karnataka",
    tagline: "The 'Dakshina Kashi' — a Vast Riverside Shiva Temple South of Mysuru",
    description: "Nanjangud, about 23 km south of Mysuru on the banks of the Kapila (Kabini) river, is revered as Dakshina Kashi — the Varanasi of the South. The name comes from the local legend that Lord Shiva drank the cosmic poison (nanju) here during the churning of the ocean, turning his throat blue and earning the name Srikanteshwara. The Srikanteshwara (Nanjundeshwara) Temple at the town centre is one of the largest ancient temple complexes in Karnataka, spread over roughly 50,000 square feet, and its fabric records successive South Indian dynasties: the innermost sanctum built by the Gangas in the 9th century, structural halls and carved pillars added by the Cholas and Hoysalas, shrines for Parvati and Narayana from the Vijayanagara era, and the finishing touches under the Wadiyars of Mysuru — including the towering nine-storey, roughly 120-foot gateway gopuram built by Queen Devajammanni in 1845. The temple's annual car festivals (rathotsava), when huge wooden chariots are pulled through the streets, are among the biggest in the region.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Srikanteshwara_Temple%2C_Nanjangud.jpg/1280px-Srikanteshwara_Temple%2C_Nanjangud.jpg",
