    description: "Himavad Gopalaswamy Betta is one of the highest peaks in the Bandipur landscape, rising to about 1,450 m (4,760 ft) in Chamarajanagar district. \"Himavad\" means mist-covered, and the hill earns the name — low cloud and fog wrap the summit through much of the year. At the top stands the Gopalaswamy Temple, an ancient shrine to Lord Krishna (Gopalaswamy) believed to date to the Cholas in the 14th century and later patronised by the Mysuru Wodeyars. Because the hill lies in the core zone of the Bandipur Tiger Reserve, there is no permanent habitation near the summit and private vehicles cannot go past the forest department check post — visitors park there and ride authorised buses to the top. On clearer spells the summit gives sweeping views over the Bandipur forest and the distant Nilgiris, and the surrounding corridor is good for spotting elephants, gaur, and birds.",
