    state: "Karnataka",
    tagline: "Hexagonal Basalt Columns on a Tiny Island Off Malpe",
    description: "St. Mary's Island is a cluster of four small islands off the coast of Malpe in Udupi district, and a recognised National Geological Monument. Its fame rests on the rare columnar basalt formations that rise straight out of the sand and shallows — clusters of roughly hexagonal stone pillars formed by ancient sub-volcanic activity, one of only a handful of places on Earth where such structures are so clearly exposed at the shore. The islands are said to have been named by Vasco da Gama, who is believed to have landed here in 1498 and dedicated the spot to St. Mary. There are no residents; access is by a scenic 20–30 minute ferry from Malpe port, and once there visitors walk the basalt zone, swim off the small white-sand Crystal Beach, and picnic under the palms. Ferries are seasonal (roughly October to May) and run only when the sea is calm.",
