    description: "Gokak Falls is where the Ghataprabha river drops around 170 feet over a broad, curving rock ledge in a horseshoe shape that has earned it comparisons to a miniature Niagara. It sits about 6 km from Gokak town in Belagavi district, in a landscape of layered sandstone cliffs and a deep gorge downstream. Above the fall stand the buildings of one of India's oldest cotton mills, powered from the river since the 1880s, and a swaying wire suspension footbridge — built in the same era — spans the river just before the drop, giving a dramatic view straight down the cascade. The falls are at their thundering best during and just after the monsoon (roughly July–October); in the dry months the flow thins to ribbons over the rock and the gorge scenery takes over. Nearby lie Godachinamalki (Markandeya) Falls and the Ghataprabha Bird Sanctuary.",
