    id: "kolar",
    name: "Kolar",
    state: "Karnataka",
    tagline: "Chola-Era Temples, the Legendary Kolar Gold Fields, and Asia's Tallest Shivalinga Nearby",
    description: "Kolar, about 70 km east of Bengaluru, layers a very long history into a compact district. Founded by the Western Ganga dynasty (who styled themselves \"Lords of Kolar\") and later ruled by the Cholas, Hoysalas, and Vijayanagara, the town centre holds the ancient Kolaramma Temple — a Dravidian goddess shrine substantially built and expanded under the Cholas in the 11th century — and the nearby Someshwara Temple with its ornate Vijayanagara-era mantapa and stepped tank. A few kilometres away, the Kolar Gold Fields (KGF) tell a different story: from 1880 the British-run mines here became among the deepest and most productive gold mines in the world, and in 1902 KGF became the first place in India to receive electricity, powered from the Shivanasamudra hydro project. The mines closed in 2001, leaving old shafts and crumbling colonial bungalows. Also in the district are the Kotilingeshwara temple complex, home to a roughly 33-metre Shivalinga said to be the tallest in Asia, and the cave-hill of Antaragange. Kolar is known too for its hand-woven Kambli wool blankets and, today, as a major tomato and milk producer.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Kolaramma_temple_at_Kolar%2C_full_view_with_main_entrance_and_altar.jpg/1280px-Kolaramma_temple_at_Kolar%2C_full_view_with_main_entrance_and_altar.jpg",
