    description: "The Dodda Basappa Temple stands just north of the village of Dambal, about 20 km from Gadag in North Karnataka. Built in the 12th century in the Western (Kalyani) Chalukya style and dedicated to Lord Shiva, it is best known for its unusual ground plan: a 24-pointed stellate (star-shaped) layout in which the sanctum walls and the soaring tiered shikhara step in and out around the whole circumference, so that the tower appears almost circular and throws a constantly shifting pattern of light and shadow across its carved surfaces. An open pillared mantapa with a seated Nandi fronts the shrine. Quiet and rarely crowded, Dambal sits on the Gadag–Hampi road and is usually visited alongside Lakkundi and Gadag's Trikuteshwara temple as part of the Chalukyan heritage circuit.",
