    description: "The Bhoga Nandeeshwara Temple sits in the village of Nandi at the base of Nandi Hills, about 60 km from Bengaluru in Chikkaballapur district, and is one of the oldest surviving stone temples in Karnataka still in worship. Its earliest shrines are attributed to the Nolambas and Western Gangas around the 9th century, and successive dynasties — Cholas, Hoysalas, and the Vijayanagara rulers — extended it over the centuries, so the complex reads as a compact museum of South Indian temple architecture. It has twin main shrines, Bhoga Nandeeshwara and Arunachaleshwara (Shiva as youth and as elder), linked by a later Uma-Maheshwara shrine, an intricately carved Vasantha (spring) or kalyana mantapa used for ritual weddings, and — its most photographed feature — the large Shringi Tirtha, a stepped temple tank ringed by pillared cloisters with the hill rising behind. It is usually paired with a Nandi Hills trip.",
