    description: "Guruvayur, a temple town near Thrissur, is built around the Sree Krishna Temple, one of the oldest and most revered Vishnu shrines in India and often called the \"Dwaraka of the South.\" The central shrine was rebuilt in 1638 CE and its sanctum walls carry 17th-century murals; the compound holds a 33.5-metre (about 70-foot) gold-plated flagstaff (dwajastambha), a 24-foot pillar of lamps (deepastambha) with thirteen tiers of oil cups, and the square Nalambalam cloister around the sanctum. The temple keeps long darshan hours (roughly 3:00 AM–12:30 PM and 4:30 PM–9:15 PM) and is famous for the Thulabharam offering, in which devotees are weighed against bananas, jaggery, sugar, or coconuts. About 3 km away, Punnathur Kotta is the temple's elephant sanctuary, home to dozens of temple elephants, and the nearby Mammiyoor and Parthasarathy temples are traditionally visited on the same trip. Major events include the February–March Utsavam, the month-long Guruvayur Ekadasi with its Carnatic music festival, and the Aanayottam elephant race.",
