    description: "Palakkad Fort, widely called Tipu's Fort, stands right in the centre of Palakkad town, a low but massive stronghold of dark laterite blocks ringed by a broad water-filled moat and a green glacis. It was built by Hyder Ali of Mysore in 1766 to control the Palakkad Gap — the great break in the Western Ghats that links the Kerala coast with Tamil Nadu — and passed to his son Tipu Sultan, seeing action in the 1783 Battle of Palakkad before falling to the British in 1790. Now protected by the Archaeological Survey of India, the fort has well-kept lawns, a children's park, an old Hanuman temple inside the walls, and an open-air auditorium (Rappadi) used for cultural programmes; the ramparts and bastions can be walked. It is an easy, central stop — a short walk from Palakkad Junction railway station and the KSRTC bus stand.",
