    tagline: "A 17th-Century Laterite Fort on a Bluff Where River Meets Sea",
    description: "Chandragiri Fort sits on a low hill about 150 feet above the water near Kasaragod, at the point where the Chandragiri river opens into the Arabian Sea. Built in the 17th century by Shivappa Nayaka of the Keladi (Ikkeri) Nayaka dynasty, it was a frontier stronghold marking the old boundary between kingdoms, and later passed through the Vijayanagara sphere, Hyder Ali, and the British East India Company. What survives today, spread over about seven acres and maintained by the Kerala Department of Archaeology, is a square circuit of weathered laterite walls with round bastions pierced for cannon, the footings of barracks and other structures, and an underground passage; excavations here have turned up cannonballs, iron tools, and pottery. The draw for most visitors is the outlook — a wide sweep over the river, the road-and-rail bridges, coconut groves, and the sea, and one of the finest sunsets in north Kerala. River houseboat cruises and small-island trips run from nearby.",
