    description: "St. Angelo Fort, also called Kannur Fort, stands on a laterite headland in Kannur town, its three massive bastions and thick red-brown walls facing straight out to the Arabian Sea. It was built in 1505 by Dom Francisco de Almeida, the first Portuguese viceroy in India, to protect Portuguese interests on the Malabar coast; the Dutch took it in 1663 and strengthened it, and the British East India Company held it from 1790 until independence, using it as a military station. Inside the walls, protected by the Archaeological Survey of India, are a moat and the line of a drawbridge, a Dutch cemetery with 17th-century gravestones, old horse stables, a vaulted arcade, British-era barracks and magazines, and cannons along the ramparts. The seaward walls give a fine view over the fishing boats of Mappila (Moppila) Bay, out toward Dharmadam Island, and one of the best sunsets in Kannur.",
