    tagline: "A Malabar Trading Town of Fort, Pier, Biryani, and the 'Three Cs'",
    description: "Thalassery, historically Tellicherry, is a seafront town in Kannur district that grew up around the spice trade and became one of the earliest British East India Company footholds on the Malabar coast. Its anchor is Tellicherry Fort, built by the Company in 1708 on a laterite headland above the sea, its gateway framed by carved figures; nearby, a 1910 pier once ran cargo out to ships anchored in the roads. The town has an outsized cultural footprint, often summed up as the \"three Cs\": cricket, which the British played here from the early days and which local families kept alive; cake and confectionery, with Mambally's bakery (well over a century old, and often cited as India's first) at its heart; and circus, the Malabar circus tradition trained in this area. Thalassery is equally famous for its biryani — a distinct, fragrant Malabar style using short-grain kaima rice — and for Herman Gundert's Illikunnu bungalow, where the first Malayalam dictionary was compiled in 1839. The cliff-top sea-view park of Overbury's Folly and the drive-in beach at Muzhappilangad are close by.",
