    description: "Aranmula, on the banks of the Pamba river in Pathanamthitta district, is a small heritage village built around the Parthasarathy Temple — a shrine to Krishna as Parthasarathy (Arjuna's charioteer) and one of the 108 Divya Desams of Vaishnava tradition. The name is said to come from the deity's idol arriving on a raft of six (aru) pieces of bamboo (mula). The temple is a fine example of Kerala wood architecture, with carved roofs and pillars showing scenes from the epics and early-18th-century murals in the sanctum, and it keeps ceremonial links with Sabarimala. Aranmula is best known for two things beyond the shrine: the Uthrattathi Vallamkali, a stately (non-competitive) procession of long Palliyodam snake boats down the Pamba during Onam, tied to the temple's Valla Sadya feast of up to 64 dishes; and the Aranmula Kannadi, a rare front-surface metal-alloy mirror still handmade by a few local families and protected as a Geographical Indication. The Vijnana Kala Vedi centre nearby teaches Kerala arts to visitors.",
