    id: "marayoor",
    name: "Marayoor",
    state: "Kerala",
    tagline: "Kerala's Only Natural Sandalwood Forest, Amid Iron-Age Dolmens",
    description: "Marayoor (Marayur), about 40 km from Munnar on the road toward Udumalpet, sits in a dry rain-shadow valley that is unlike anywhere else in Kerala. It is the only part of the state with naturally growing sandalwood — tens of thousands of trees, prized for their quality, managed by the Forest Department with a regeneration plot and a sandalwood depot open for visitor education. The same hills hold one of South India's densest concentrations of megalithic monuments: clusters of dolmens (muniyaras), each four upright slabs capped by a fifth stone, roughly 2,000 years old and dating to the Iron Age, alongside prehistoric rock paintings in shelters at Ezhuthu Guha, Attala, Manala, and Kovilkadavu (the Attala shelter alone has more than 90 painted motifs, above 1,500 m). Marayoor is also known for \"Marayoor sarkara,\" a dark solid jaggery with very high sugar content made across the valley as a cottage industry, and for the millet-based cuisine of its Muthuvan and Hill Pulaya communities.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Dolmens_%40_Marayoor_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg",
