    description: "The Keshava (Chennakeshava) Temple at Somanathapura village, about 35 km east of Mysuru, is often called the most complete and refined surviving Hoysala temple. It was built in 1268 CE by Somanatha Dandanayaka, a military commander of the Hoysala king Narasimha III, and follows a trikuta plan — three shrines opening off a common pillared hall — the whole set on a raised, star-shaped platform (jagati) that visitors circle to read the sculpture. Carved from soft chloritic schist (soapstone) that hardens with exposure, the outer walls run in horizontal bands of elephants, horsemen, scrollwork, and scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and Bhagavata Purana, topped by rows of intricately worked towers. The original idols were lost to later invasion and there is no active worship, so the ASI-protected monument can be studied up close without restriction. In 2023 it was inscribed by UNESCO as part of the \"Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas,\" together with Belur and Halebidu. It pairs naturally with Talakadu and the Shivanasamudra falls.",
