    description: "Ranganathittu, on the Cauvery about 16–19 km from Mysuru near Srirangapatna, is one of India's most rewarding and accessible bird reserves. A line of small wooded islands and rocky outcrops in the river — their shape shaped by an old check-dam built by the Mysore Wodeyars in the 17th century — gives waterbirds a predator-safe place to roost and breed, and the ornithologist Salim Ali was instrumental in getting it declared a sanctuary in 1940. From the boat jetty, short shared-boat rides slip between the islands past dense colonies of painted storks, spot-billed pelicans, open-billed storks, cormorants, egrets, herons, and river terns, with large mugger crocodiles hauled out on the rocks and, in winter, migratory ducks and waders. The sanctuary is open roughly 9 AM to 6 PM, and the breeding-and-nesting season (about June to November) is the most spectacular time to visit.",
