    description: "Banavasi, a quiet village on the Varada river in Uttara Kannada about 20–25 km from Sirsi, is generally regarded as the oldest town in Karnataka: it was the first capital of the Kadamba dynasty, the earliest indigenous ruling house of the region, from around the 4th century CE, and it appears in far older records still. Its living heart is the Madhukeshwara Temple, dedicated to Shiva, whose sanctum holds a distinctive honey-coloured (madhu) linga and which faces a large monolithic Nandi; the complex grew across the Kadamba, Chalukya, Hoysala, and Vijayanagara periods and holds fine stone carving, a carved stone cot, and old inscriptions. The Kannada poet Pampa wrote of Banavasi with deep affection, and the village still honours that heritage each year with Kadambotsava, a cultural festival of classical music and dance, folk performances, craft stalls, and heritage walks held around the temple. It is an easy, unhurried half-day trip from Sirsi.",
