    tagline: "A White-Cliff Hilltop Temple in a Tiger Reserve Where Two Ghats Meet",
    description: "The Biligiriranga Hills — BR Hills — rise in Chamarajanagar district at the meeting point of the Western and Eastern Ghats, and the range takes its name from the Biligiri Ranganatha Swamy Temple that sits on a sheer white cliff near the crest (biligiri means \"white cliff\"). The whole massif is protected as the Biligiri Ranganatha Swamy Temple (BRT) Tiger Reserve, whose habitats shift from dry deciduous forest through moist deciduous to shola-and-grassland, and which holds tigers, leopards, elephants, gaur, sambar, dhole (wild dogs), sloth bear, four-horned antelope, and more than 200 bird species. It is the traditional home of the Soliga, an indigenous forest community. Wildlife safaris and stays are centred on K. Gudi (Kyathadevaragudi); the temple draws large crowds for its annual April festival. The reserve is at its most alive with the rains, roughly June to October.",
