    description: "Mekedatu — Kannada for \"goat's leap\" — is a deep, narrow gorge on the Kaveri river in Ramanagara district, about 90 km from Bengaluru, where the broad river funnels into a chasm only a few metres wide between sculpted grey rocks, so tight that legend says a goat once jumped across it. A little upstream at Sangama, the Arkavathi river joins the Kaveri; in the dry months you cross the shallow confluence by tractor-trailer or on foot, and in the monsoon the crossing closes as the water rises. The setting is dry scrub-forest hills with basking soft-shell turtles and good birdlife, and it links to the Cauvery Wildlife Sanctuary and the fishing-and-kayaking camps at Bheemeshwari and Galibore. It is a popular day trip from Bengaluru, best in the cooler, drier months.",
