    description: "Beypore, about 10 km south of Kozhikode (Calicut) city where the Chaliyar river meets the Arabian Sea, was for centuries one of the Malabar Coast's most important ports and a node on the maritime spice route, trading with Arab, Chinese, and European merchants; Tipu Sultan is credited with developing the harbour further. Its lasting fame is the uru — the large wooden dhow (trading ship) that the Khalasis and master shipwrights of Beypore have built entirely by hand, without formal drawings, for well over a thousand years. The yards still take commissions, mostly from the Gulf, and visitors can watch a hull taking shape. Along the shore are Beypore Beach and a long paved walkway out over the water, the striped Beypore Lighthouse, and the old port area. The Beypore International Water Fest (usually held over the year-end) brings kayaking, bamboo rafting, sailboat rides, and regattas to the rivermouth.",
