    description: "Parambikulam Tiger Reserve lies in the Sungam range of the Western Ghats in Palakkad district, hemmed in on three sides by Tamil Nadu's Anamalai reserve, so that the protected forest continues across the state line. Within roughly 640 sq km of evergreen, moist and dry deciduous forest and grassland live Bengal tigers, leopards, elephants, gaur, sloth bear, sambar, spotted deer, and the endemic lion-tailed macaque and Nilgiri langur, along with great hornbills, Malabar pied hornbills, and crested serpent eagles. Three linked reservoirs — Parambikulam, Thunakadavu, and Peruvaripallam — sit at its centre. The reserve is known for the Kannimara Teak, among the oldest and largest living teak trees anywhere, and it is home to the Kadar, Malasar, Muduvar, and Mala Malasar tribal communities, whose culture is presented at a Tribal Heritage museum. Access is strictly regulated: visitors enter through the Anappady check post from the Pollachi side, numbers are capped, and all activity — bus safaris, guided treks such as the Kariyanshola nature trail, boating, and stays in tree-houts, tented niches, and the island nest — is booked through the reserve's eco-tourism system.",
