    description: "Kuruvadweep, or Kuruva Island, is a cluster of uninhabited islets — about 950 acres in all — set in the Kabini (Kabani) river in the Mananthavady area of Wayanad, near the Kerala–Karnataka border. It is a protected evergreen river delta managed by the Kerala Forest Department, and reaching it is part of the experience: there is no bridge, so visitors cross the river on rafts run by the Forest Department's Vana Samrakshana Samithi together with the District Tourism Promotion Council. On the island, movement is on foot along marked forest trails, over temporary bamboo footbridges between the islets, with bamboo rafting on the quieter channels. The undisturbed woods hold rare orchids, herbs and butterflies and a good range of forest birds. Because it sits in the Kabini basin, Kuruva is closed to visitors through the monsoon (roughly June to September) when the river runs high, and it is open otherwise from about 9:00 AM to 4:30 PM, with Mondays off. Plan on at least three hours.",
