    description: "Pookode (Pookkot) Lake is a small natural freshwater lake in the evergreen hills of Wayanad, near Lakkidi and about 3 km from Vythiri. At around 770 m above sea level it is described as both the highest-altitude and the smallest freshwater lake in Kerala, covering roughly 8.5 hectares with a maximum depth of about 6.5 m. Its outline is often said to resemble the physical map of India, and its shallows carry blue water lilies. The lake is the source of the Panamaram river, which flows down the valley to join the Kabini. It is a managed day-use spot run with the District Tourism Promotion Council: pedal boats and rowing boats for a circuit of the water, a small freshwater aquarium (including the locally endemic fish Pethia pookodensis, which gave the lake its name), a children's park, and a craft-and-spice shopping centre selling bamboo and coconut-husk ware, honey and herbal products. Cool, misty winter mornings are the classic time to visit.",
