    tagline: "The Palpayasam Temple — Krishna as Parthasarathy, in Classic Kerala Wood and Tile",
    description: "The Ambalappuzha Sree Krishna Temple stands in a quiet part of Ambalappuzha, about 14 km south of Alappuzha (Alleppey) town. Built by Pooradam Thirunal Devanarayanan Thampuran, the ruler of the Chembakassery kingdom, between roughly the 15th and 17th centuries, it is counted among the seven great Vaishnava temples of Kerala and is designed in pure Kerala style — tiled and thatched roofs, a pillared chuttambalam with old wall paintings, and a large temple tank. The deity is Krishna in the form of Parthasarathy, Arjuna's charioteer. The temple is famous across India for its palpayasam, a sweet milk-and-rice pudding offered to the deity and distributed to devotees around midday; a well-known legend ties the offering to a game of chess between the king and Krishna in disguise. Ambalappuzha is also remembered as the place where the poet Kunchan Nambiar first performed Ottanthullal, the satirical solo dance-narrative. Entry to the temple is for Hindus only, following a Kerala temple dress code.",
