    tagline: "A Renowned Devi Temple Near Kochi, Worshipped Morning to Night in Three Forms",
    description: "The Chottanikkara Bhagavathy Temple is one of Kerala's most visited Devi shrines, in Chottanikkara village about 18–20 km southeast of Kochi (Ernakulam), near Tripunithura. Believed to date back to around the 10th century, it centres on the goddess Bhagavathy (Rajarajeswari), worshipped through the day in three moods — as Saraswathi in the morning, Lakshmi at midday and Durga in the evening. Below the main temple is the Keezhkkavu shrine of Bhadrakali, the focus of the evening guruthi puja. Chottanikkara has a long-standing reputation as a place people come to seek relief from mental distress and what tradition describes as affliction by spirits; near the Keezhkkavu is a paala tree studded with iron nails said to have been driven in by devotees. The temple's signature festival is the Makam Thozhal, on the Makam star in the Malayalam month of Kumbham (about mid-February to mid-March). Entry is for Hindus only, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
