    description: "The Cheraman Juma Masjid stands at Methala near Kodungallur, in Thrissur district, on land that was part of the ancient Muziris port. It is widely regarded as the oldest mosque in India, traditionally dated to 629 CE and associated with Malik Bin Dinar. The story tied to it is that of a Chera king (the \"Cheraman Perumal\") who saw the moon split, learned from Arab traders that this was a miracle of the Prophet Muhammad, sailed to Arabia and embraced Islam; on his instruction, Malik Bin Dinar and companions later built the mosque here. What sets it apart is its form: rather than domes and minarets, the mosque follows traditional Kerala architecture, with white walls and tiered gabled tiled roofs that closely resemble a Hindu temple or a nalukettu house. Inside are an old carved wooden pulpit and a brass oil lamp that devotees believe has burned continuously for centuries. A major conservation project completed in 2022 restored much of the traditional roofline. People of all faiths visit the mosque; it is part of the Muziris Heritage circuit.",
