    tagline: "Kerala's Tallest Lighthouse and the Laterite Ruins of \"Dutch Quilon\"",
    description: "Thangassery is a seaside quarter of Kollam town, on a cape jutting into the Arabian Sea, layered with the traces of Kerala's colonial-era trade. The Portuguese built a fort here in the early 16th century (about 1518–19); the Dutch took it in 1661 and it passed to the British in 1795, who nicknamed the enclave \"Dutch Quilon\". What remains today is a cluster of heritage: the moss-grown laterite ruins of St Thomas Fort, an old Portuguese cemetery, 18th-century churches including the Infant Jesus Cathedral, a colonial canal, and the site of the ancient port of Quilon. Rising above it all is the Thangassery Lighthouse, built by the British in 1902 — at about 144 ft, the tallest in Kerala, its spiral red-and-white bands visible for miles. Visitors can climb it (by stairs, with a lift also provided) for a wide view of the coast, harbour and breakwater. The rocky shore and the newer Breakwater tourism park make it a popular evening spot for Kollam residents.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Thangassery_Lighthouse_Kollam_Kerala_Mar22_A7C_01634.jpg/1280px-Thangassery_Lighthouse_Kollam_Kerala_Mar22_A7C_01634.jpg",
