    description: "Nilambur, a forested town on the Chaliyar river in Malappuram district, is synonymous with teak. It is where systematic teak planting in India began: Conolly's Plot, raised from 1846 under H.V. Conolly, the Collector of Malabar, with the plantation work led by Chathu Menon, is recognised as the world's oldest teak plantation. The original planting covered a large area; part was felled during the Second World War for Allied timber, and a core of about 5.7 acres was set aside as a research reserve in 1993. It is reached on foot across a swaying hanging bridge over the Chaliyar. Close by is the KFRI Teak Museum, set up in 1995 by the Kerala Forest Department and Kerala Forest Research Institute — the first museum in the world devoted to teak — with exhibits on the tree's biology, history and use, plus a butterfly garden, a bamboo grove and a medicinal-plant garden. The wider Nilambur area has teak reserves, the Kovilakam (palace) clusters of the old Nilambur royal family, and forest edges leading toward Nedumkayam and the Western Ghats.",
