    description: "The Vaikom Mahadeva Temple sits amid the backwaters near Vembanad Lake in Vaikom, Kottayam district, and is counted among the oldest and most revered Shiva temples in Kerala. Its Shiva Linga is held by tradition to date from the Treta Yuga, and the temple is unusual in Kerala for having an oval-plan sreekovil (sanctum), stone-built including its roof. Vaikom is one of three temples — with Ettumanoor and Kaduthuruthy — that devout pilgrims try to worship at on the same day, a circuit said to equal a visit to Kailasa. It is known for its prathal, a large-scale free feeding of devotees, and for the 12-day Vaikathashtami festival in the Malayalam month of Vrischikam (Nov–Dec), which ends with a grand elephant procession. Vaikom also holds a place in modern Indian history: the Vaikom Satyagraha of 1924–25, a non-violent campaign for the right of all castes to use the public roads around the temple, drew national attention and Mahatma Gandhi's involvement. Entry to the temple is for Hindus, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
