    state: "Kerala",
    tagline: "A Forest-Grove Serpent Temple Led by a Woman Priest",
    description: "The Mannarasala Sree Nagaraja Temple stands in a shaded grove near Haripad in Alappuzha district, and is the best known of Kerala's serpent (naga) shrines. The main deity is Nagaraja, the serpent king, held to combine the spirit of Shiva and the form of Vishnu, with Sarpa Yakshi and Naga Yakshi as consorts. Along the wooded paths and among the roots of the old trees stand many thousands of stone serpent images — by tradition around 30,000 — left by devotees over generations. Mannarasala is unusual in being led by a woman priest, the Mannarasala Amma, of the Brahmin family whose ancestral house (the Illom) adjoins the temple and manages it. Couples hoping for children perform the uruli kamazhthal ritual, placing an inverted bronze vessel in the temple and returning to turn it upright after a child is born, and the temple gives out a turmeric powder believed to have healing properties. The big annual festival is the Ayilyam, on the Ayilyam star in the Malayalam month of Thulam (about Sep–Nov), when the serpent idols are carried in procession to the Illom. Entry is for Hindus, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
