    description: "Ramakkalmedu is a hill viewpoint on the eastern rim of the Idukki highlands, near Nedumkandam and close to the Kerala–Tamil Nadu border. A tall, pillar-like rock (about 300 m) stands at the edge of a sheer escarpment, and from the grassy shoulder around it the land falls away to the plains of Tamil Nadu — the towns of Cumbum, Theni, Bodinayakkanur and their patchwork of villages spread out far below. It is one of the windiest places in Asia: a strong, steady wind blows here almost constantly, which is why the Kerala government has built wind farms along the ridge generating over 10 MW. The name is linked to a Ramayana legend, with marks in the rock said to be Rama's footprints. The site's landmark is the giant Kuravan and Kurathi statue — a seated tribal couple with their child, put up in 2005, honouring the two figures of local legend credited with helping engineers find the spot for the Idukki Dam. There are short walks, unusual rock formations, and, on the wind, occasional paragliding events.",
