    description: "The Idukki Arch Dam is a double-curvature parabolic arch dam built across the Periyar in a narrow gorge between two granite hills traditionally called Kuravanmala and Kurathimala, in Idukki district. At about 168 m (roughly 550 ft) high it is one of the tallest arch dams in Asia and was among the first of its kind on the continent; the idea was first proposed by W.H. John in 1932, and the completed project was dedicated by Prime Minister Indira Gandhi in 1976. Idukki is the storage dam of a three-dam system — with the Cheruthoni and Kulamavu dams — that impounds a vast reservoir (around 2 billion tonnes of water) feeding the underground Moolamattam power station, the largest hydro plant in Kerala at 780 MW. The dam is not normally open to the public: it is opened for visitors only for a limited season (usually around April–June), on set hours and closed on one weekday, with tickets, buggy rides along the crest and boat rides on the reservoir. Photography and phones are restricted on and around the dam for security. Even when the dam itself is closed, the reservoir and the surrounding hill viewpoints (Kolumban, Hill View Park near Cheruthoni) are worth the trip.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Idukki_dam_arch.jpg",
