    description: "Kadalundi Bird Sanctuary sits where the Kadalundi river spreads into a cluster of small islands and mudflats before joining the Arabian Sea, on the border of Kozhikode and Malappuram districts, about 25 km south of Kozhikode. It forms part of the Kadalundi–Vallikkunnu Community Reserve, established in 2007 as Kerala's first community reserve, jointly protected by local people and the Forest Department. The estuary's mangroves and tidal flats draw more than 60 species of migratory birds — terns, gulls, herons, whimbrels, sandpipers, plovers, redshanks and greenshanks among them — that arrive roughly from November to April from Siberia, Central Asia and Europe, alongside resident kingfishers, darters, Brahminy kites and Malabar hornbills; otters and jackals live in the mangroves. Low tide, when the mudflats are exposed and the birds feed, and early morning are the best times. Visits are on foot along the shore and bunds, from a low hillock viewpoint, or by small boat; the area has a long history as a Chera-period port.",
