    description: "Peermade — Peermedu — is a hill town at about 915 m in the Idukki high ranges, roughly 40 km short of Kumily/Thekkady on the road up from Kottayam. It takes its name from Peer Mohammed, a Sufi saint said to have settled here, and was a favourite summer retreat of the Travancore royal family: the old Summer Palace (now a government guest house) and Ammachikottaram, the residence of Maharani Sethu Lakshmi Bai, still stand. The 19th-century missionary Henry Baker Jr. recognised the area's potential for plantations, and today the hills are covered in tea, coffee, cardamom, rubber and eucalyptus, with estate tours on offer. The surrounding country is good for gentle trekking, cycling and horse riding, and short drives reach viewpoints and sights — Parunthupara (\"Eagle Rock\"), the Pattumala church and hill, Panchalimedu, Grandanam/Grandis, and the adventure-tourism base of Kuttikkanam a few km away. It is much quieter and cooler than nearby Thekkady, and often misty.",
