    description: "The Thirunelli Maha Vishnu Temple sits in a quiet valley at the foot of the Brahmagiri hills in northern Wayanad, about 30 km from Mananthavady near the Kerala–Karnataka border, reached by a road through the Tholpetty forest. Believed to be more than a thousand years old — copper-plate inscriptions and coins from the 9th–11th centuries have been found here — it is built almost entirely of granite, with about thirty carved stone pillars around the shrine, big square granite slabs paving the floor, and a Kerala-style tiled sanctum; an old stone aqueduct still carries spring water into the temple. The deity is Vishnu as Chaturbhuja. Thirunelli is known as the \"Kashi of the South\" (Sahyamala / Amara Kshetram) and, like Gaya, is a place people come to perform pitru bali, the rite for departed ancestors, standing in the sacred Papanasini stream that runs down from Brahmagiri about a kilometre away; the temple tank, Panchatheertham, has a boulder marked as Vishnu's footprint. Entry is for Hindus, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
