    id: "anchuthengu-fort",
    name: "Anchuthengu Fort (Anjengo Fort)",
    state: "Kerala",
    tagline: "The English East India Company's First Foothold in Kerala, on a Sea-and-Backwater Spit",
    description: "Anchuthengu — anglicised as \"Anjengo\" — is a narrow strip of land between the Arabian Sea and a backwater lagoon, about 12 km south of Varkala in Thiruvananthapuram district. It is where the English East India Company established its first trading post on the Kerala coast: a factory from 1684, and then a fort, built between about 1695 and 1699 with the permission of the Rani (queen) of Attingal, along with a monopoly on the local pepper trade. The compact fort is essentially a walled bastion of high laterite ramparts with lookout points, enclosing a grassy courtyard and the ruined shells of barracks and storehouses; from the walls there are wide views of the sea. It was a signalling station and ammunition store during the Anglo–Mysore wars. In 1721 came the Attingal Outbreak (or Attingal revolt), when local people attacked an English party and besieged the fort, killing around 140 — often described as one of the first organised revolts against the English in India. An old European cemetery with 17th–18th-century tombstones lies nearby, along with the Anchuthengu lighthouse. Entry to the ASI-protected fort is generally free and open during the day.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Anjengo_Fort%2C_Kerala.jpg/1280px-Anjengo_Fort%2C_Kerala.jpg",
