    description: "Chinnar Wildlife Sanctuary covers about 90 sq km on the dry eastern side of the Western Ghats in Idukki district, on the Munnar–Udumalpet road (SH17) where Kerala meets Tamil Nadu's Anamalai (Indira Gandhi) reserve. It sits in a rain shadow — only a couple of months of rain a year — so instead of the usual Kerala evergreens it holds dry thorn scrub, deciduous forest and grassland, with a bright green ribbon of riparian forest along the Chinnar and Pambar rivers. It is the only place in Kerala with the endangered grizzled giant squirrel, the sanctuary's flagship animal, and it runs a rehabilitation programme for the Indian star tortoise. Mugger crocodiles live in the rivers; the sanctuary also has Nilgiri tahr, elephants, gaur, sambar, leopard and tiger, and more than 225 bird species. Muthuva and Hill Pulaya tribal communities live inside and take part in the eco-tourism, which is run by the Forest Department: guided nature walks, the Thoovanam waterfall trek, river-side trekking, cultural visits, and stays in tree houses and log houses. Entry needs a ticket and, for treks, a guide.",
