    description: "Meesapulimala, at about 2,640 m in the high ranges of Idukki near Munnar, is the second-highest peak in South India after Anamudi. The name joins meesa (moustache) and pulimala (tiger hill), for the whisker-like line of its ridges. The landscape is a mosaic of rolling grassland, steep cliffs and pockets of shola forest, and on most days clouds pour across the slopes, hiding and revealing rock faces and valleys. It is reached only as an organised trek: most routes start from the Rhodo Valley base — named for its rhododendrons — on the Kolukkumalai side, roughly 20–25 km from Munnar, and the walk climbs over several ridges to the summit, where clear weather gives long views of Munnar, Suryanelli, the tea country and the Kerala–Tamil Nadu border. The trek is run with permits and guides through the Munnar tourism/forest set-up, often as a day hike or with a night at the Rhodo Valley camp. October to March gives the clearest, most stable weather.",
