    description: "Neyyar Wildlife Sanctuary wraps around the reservoir of the Neyyar Dam, on the southern tip of the Western Ghats about 30 km east of Thiruvananthapuram via Kattakada. The dam was built in 1958 and the sanctuary — roughly 128 sq km — was notified the same year. It is an easy half-day trip from the city and packs in several draws: a deer park, a Lion Safari Park where a small enclosure is toured by vehicle, and a Crocodile Rehabilitation and Research Centre (a crocodile farm started here in 1977). Boats run on the reservoir, ringed by forest with a pointed peak on the skyline. The wilder interior holds elephant, gaur, sloth bear, sambar, Nilgiri langur, leopard and (rarely) tiger, with over 170 bird species; Kani tribal communities live in the sanctuary. Neyyar is also the base for the tough trek to Agasthyakoodam (about 1,868 m), which runs only in a short season (roughly January–March) with a Forest Department permit. The internationally known Sivananda Yoga Vedanta ashram sits on the reservoir's edge.",
