    description: "Thirunavaya, on the north bank of the Bharathapuzha (the Nila) near Tirur in Malappuram district, is built around the Navamukunda Temple — one of the oldest Vishnu temples in Kerala and one of the 108 Divya Desams, with the deity worshipped as Navamukunda Perumal. An old Kerala-style structure (a major renovation is credited to the Vettath Raja centuries ago), it forms a trio with Siva and Brahma temples across the river. Thirunavaya was the venue of the Mamankam, a great fair-cum-assembly held on the river once every twelve years from at least the 8th century, watched by the Zamorin of Kozhikode from a stone platform, the Pazhukka Mandapam, still in the temple's south-east corner; the Mamankam is remembered for the chaver warriors who died trying to reach the Zamorin. Today the site is one of Kerala's most important places for bali tarpanam — offerings to departed ancestors — performed daily on the riverbank and, above all, on Karkidaka Vavu (the new-moon day of the Malayalam month Karkidakam), when very large crowds gather. Entry to the temple is for Hindus, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
