    state: "Kerala",
    tagline: "Kerala's First Bird Sanctuary — a Lowland Forest Salim Ali Called the Richest in Peninsular India",
    description: "The Dr Salim Ali Bird Sanctuary at Thattekkad lies about 12 km from Kothamangalam in Ernakulam district, in a patch of low-lying \"flat forest\" (that is what \"Thattekkad\" means) held between two branches of the Periyar, near the Bhoothathankettu dam. The ornithologist Salim Ali surveyed it in 1933 and rated it the richest bird habitat in peninsular India; on his recommendation it was made Kerala's first bird sanctuary in 1983 and named after him. In roughly 25 sq km of semi-evergreen, evergreen and moist-deciduous forest with old plantations, birders record 270–300+ species, among them sought-after ones like the Sri Lanka (Ceylon) frogmouth, Sri Lanka bay owl, Malabar trogon, grey-headed bulbul and several hornbills, plus a strong show of butterflies and dragonflies. It is explored on foot along birding trails, usually with a local guide, with boating on the Periyar/Bhoothathankettu water; there is also a small deer park, butterfly park and rehabilitation centre. October to March is the general season, and February–April is prized for breeding activity.",
