    tagline: "The Head Kali Shrine of Malabar, and the Fierce Kodungallur Bharani Festival",
    description: "The Sree Kurumba Bhagavathy Temple stands at the centre of Kodungallur town, in Thrissur district on the site of the ancient port of Muziris. One of the oldest temples in Kerala, it is dedicated to Bhadrakali (a form of Mahakali) and is regarded as the head of the 64 Bhadrakali kavus of Malabar. It is a low, classic Kerala-style structure of tiled roofs and pillared halls around a large sandy courtyard and a temple tank. The temple is best known for the Kodungallur Bharani, a month-long festival running from the Bharani star in Kumbham to a week past the Bharani star in Meenam (about February to April). It is one of Kerala's most intense rituals: it opens with the Kozhikkallu Moodal, involves the loud, deliberately transgressive Bharani songs, and climaxes in the Kavutheendal, when an enormous congregation of komarams (oracles) storms the temple in a trance, striking their foreheads with swords. Outside the festival it is a calm, working temple. Entry is for Hindus, with a Kerala temple dress code.",
