    description: "Barkur, a small town near Udupi on the coastal plain, was for centuries the capital of Tulunadu: the seat of the Alupa dynasty from around the 10th–11th centuries, and later an important administrative centre under Vijayanagara. Tradition holds it once had 365 temples — one for each day, which the pious Alupa kings are said to have visited in turn — and around forty temples and shrines survive today, alongside the low, overgrown remains of Barkur Fort. Among the sites are the 8th-century Panchalingeshwara temple, the 12th-century Kattale Basadi (a Jain temple whose stone interior stays so dark it is named for the darkness, kattale), and shrines blending Hindu, Jain and early Islamic history. It is a quiet place of paddy fields, laterite ruins and scattered monuments rather than a single big draw — an easy heritage half-day from Udupi.",
