    description: "Shivagange is a granite monolith hill near Dobbaspet, about 55 km northwest of Bengaluru toward Tumakuru, rising to roughly 1,368 m. Seen from different sides it is said to look like a Shiva linga, a cobra, a Nandi bull and a Ganesha, and it is both a pilgrimage site and a popular day-trek. At its foot and along the climb are the Gavi Gangadhareshwara temple, a 16th-century Dravidian shrine partly built into a natural cavern with a large monolithic Nandi carved from a single rock; the Olakal Theertha, a sacred spring reached by squeezing an arm into a narrow rock crevice; the Shanthala Drop, a sheer cliff edge; and smaller shrines and mantapas. At Maha Shivaratri the temple draws big crowds for the tradition in which ghee smeared on the linga is said to turn to butter. The path to the summit is steep, with sections cut into bare rock and fitted with chains and railings, and takes about 30–45 minutes each way. Troops of bold monkeys along the route routinely snatch food and loose belongings.",
