    description: "Kavaratti is the administrative headquarters of Lakshadweep and its most developed island, roughly 6 km long with a broad, calm western lagoon. It has about 52 mosques scattered across it — the finest is the Ujra (Jama-Ath) mosque, with an ornately carved ceiling and a well whose water is held to have curative powers. For visitors the draw is the water: the sheltered lagoon is one of the best places in India to try scuba diving for the first time (the Dolphin Dive Centre operates here, on reefs with sea fans, turtles, soft corals, reef sharks and rays), along with snorkelling, glass-bottom-boat rides, kayaking and windsurfing. There is also a small marine aquarium. Kavaratti is reached by boat from Agatti in the fair season, on the SPORTS \"Taratashi\" resort package, or as a stop on the Samudram cruise ship. An entry permit is required, alcohol is prohibited, and you may not leave the lagoon without a SPORTS escort.",
