    description: "Kalpeni is an atoll in the Laccadive group — the main inhabited island of Kalpeni, plus the smaller islets of Tilakkam and Pitti, and the uninhabited Cheriyam nearby. Its most distinctive feature is a huge bank of coral debris piled along the eastern and south-eastern shore, thrown up by a violent storm in 1847. The lagoon is broad, shallow and clear, ideal for reef-walking, swimming, snorkelling, and kayak and sailboat trips, and Koomel Beach has two bathing huts with change rooms. The old Moideen mosque, its front rebuilt around 350 years ago and set among ponds near the sea, is a local landmark. Kalpeni also holds a place in the islands' social history as the place where girls first went to school when women's education was still frowned upon. It is visited by ship, on the Samudram cruise and related packages; an entry permit is required and alcohol is prohibited. The nearby Pitti islet is a protected seabird breeding sanctuary and is not a landing spot for visitors.",
