    description: "Minicoy (Maliku) is the southernmost island of Lakshadweep, lying on its own about 200 km south of the main group and close to the Maldives — and it is culturally distinct from the rest of the archipelago. Crescent-shaped and around 10.6 km long (the second largest island after Andrott), it wraps around one of the largest lagoons in Lakshadweep, with the small uninhabited islet of Viringili on its southern side. The island is a cluster of 11 villages, each with an elected head (Bodukaka); people speak Mahl (Dhivehi), and Minicoy is famous for its \"Lava\" dance and its long, brightly painted communal rowing boats, the jahadhoni, raced between villages. Landmarks include a tall British-era lighthouse (built 1885, ~50 m) and a busy tuna fishery with a canning/processing factory. There is a small tourist home and a few cottages, bathing huts, water sports and a dive centre. Minicoy is reached only by ship — on the Samudram cruise and the \"Swaying Palm\" package — as it has no airstrip or helipad; an entry permit is required and alcohol is prohibited.",
