    tagline: "Tens of Thousands of Years of Rock Art in the Vindhyan Hills",
    description: "The Bhimbetka rock shelters lie in dense deciduous forest on the sandstone hills of the Vindhyan range, about 45 km south-east of Bhopal in Raisen district. Around 760 natural shelters are scattered over seven hills, of which roughly 500 carry paintings — a near-continuous record of human life from the Mesolithic period through historic times, with some of the deep cup-marks (cupules) in the Auditorium Cave far older still. The images, done in ochre, red and white, show hunting scenes, dancing, animals (bison, deer, elephants, tigers), riders, and later battle and religious motifs. It was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2003. A marked visitor trail links about 15 of the best shelters, among them the vast Auditorium Rock, Zoo Rock (the most densely painted), Boar Rock and Turtle Rock. It pairs naturally with the nearby Bhojpur Shiva temple, under an hour away.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/A_painting_on_the_walls_of_cave_in_Bhimbetka_rock_shelter%2C_Madhya_Pradesh.jpg/1280px-A_painting_on_the_walls_of_cave_in_Bhimbetka_rock_shelter%2C_Madhya_Pradesh.jpg",
