    description: "At Bhedaghat, about 20 km from Jabalpur, the Narmada river runs for a couple of kilometres between sheer cliffs of pale, cream-and-grey marble (dolomitic limestone) that rise up to around 30 m — a canyon best seen from a rowing boat, and most magical on a full-moon night when the marble seems to glow. The stone has been quarried and hand-carved here for generations, and boatmen point out shapes in the rock along the way, including the narrow \"Bandar Kudni\" gap. Just downstream the river suddenly drops in the Dhuandhar (\"smoke cascade\") Falls, a broad, roaring fall that throws up a permanent cloud of spray; a ropeway crosses the gorge beside it. On a hill above the ghats stands the circular 10th-century Chausath Yogini temple, ringed by carved stone goddess figures. Boat rides run in the calm months; in the monsoon the falls are at their most powerful but boating in the gorge is suspended when the river is high.",
