    id: "pench-national-park",
    name: "Pench National Park & Tiger Reserve",
    state: "Madhya Pradesh",
    tagline: "The \"Jungle Book\" Forest on the MP–Maharashtra Border",
    description: "Pench Tiger Reserve straddles the Madhya Pradesh–Maharashtra border in Seoni and Chhindwara districts, named for the Pench river that runs through it. Its dry and moist deciduous teak forest and grassy meadows are the country that Rudyard Kipling had in mind for The Jungle Book — the Seoni hills and the area around Alikatta appear in the story — and the reserve markets itself as the \"Land of the Jungle Book.\" It holds a healthy tiger population along with leopard, dhole (Indian wild dog), gaur, cheetal, sambar, nilgai and four-horned antelope, and more than 280 bird species including four kinds of vulture. Jeep safaris run from gates on the MP side (Turia is the main one, plus Karmajhiri and Jamtara), booked online through the MP Forest Department. The park is open roughly 1 October to 30 June and closes in the monsoon; October to February is the pleasantest time. Nagpur, about 90 km away, is the usual gateway.",
    image: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Tiger_of_Turia_Core_Zone_-_Pench_National_Park.jpg/1280px-Tiger_of_Turia_Core_Zone_-_Pench_National_Park.jpg",
