    description: "Panna Tiger Reserve lies in the Vindhya hills of Panna and Chhatarpur districts, about 25 km from Khajuraho, with the Ken river cutting gorges through its dry teak-and-grassland plateau. Its tigers famously died out around 2009; a reintroduction programme begun that year re-established a breeding population, and Panna is now cited as one of the country's clearest tiger-recovery successes. Alongside tigers the reserve has leopard, sloth bear, wild dog, wolf, cheetal, sambar, nilgai and chinkara, seven species of vulture and more than 200 birds, and the Ken shelters mugger crocodile and the rare fish-eating gharial. Jeep safaris run from the Madla and Hinauta gates, and there are boat rides on the Ken; the Pandav Falls (inside the park) and the Raneh Falls canyon on the Ken (nearby, sometimes called the \"Grand Canyon of India\") are the other draws. The park is open roughly October to June and closes in the monsoon.",
