    description: "Kuno National Park lies in Sheopur district in the far north-west of Madhya Pradesh, on the meandering Kuno river near the Rajasthan border, in dry deciduous forest of kardhai, salai and khair with broad grasslands — country that was originally prepared as a second home for the Asiatic lion. It is now the site of Project Cheetah: eight cheetahs from Namibia were released here on 17 September 2022 and a further group from South Africa in February 2023, the first time the cat has lived in India since it was declared extinct in the country in 1952. The park also has leopard, striped hyena, jackal, sloth bear, chital, sambar, nilgai, chinkara and more than 120 bird species, with marsh crocodile in the Kuno river. A limited cheetah safari operates in the Ahera tourism zone, with jeep safaris booked online through the MP Forest Department; open roughly October to June and closed in the monsoon. Sightings of free-ranging cheetahs are not guaranteed and the programme is still evolving.",
